  "findings": [
    "Legacy passes all original 18 cases, but the new separate-topics test exposes an obsolete 3000 amount still phrased as current after correction to 5000. The recorded paragraph separately adds the corrected amount, creating ambiguity; this is not merely a historical-value regex false positive.",
    "Incremental passes 17/21 twice, versus legacy 20/21 twice. Pending-state loss in zh_phased_correction and a completed-state update in en_incremental_completion are deferred by existing validation, leaving the old frontier.",
    "The incremental long-context case actually commits prose omitting original audio retention, human modification, noise and boundary topics. Valid original-source citations alone do not prove semantic coverage. The external corpus detects this loss.",
    "Both modes fail the new obsolete-budget assertion. Incremental article compression is not a substitute for an entity/item/owner/deadline/status ledger.",
    "Incremental uses 9321 input/1467 output tokens and 35 requests (5 repairs) versus legacy 6952/1495 and 30 requests (1 repair) in each trial. Costs include failed and repeated batches; shorter fresh-source input does not imply lower total cost.",
    "Incremental process totals 56.625/55.900 s versus legacy 53.548/53.512 s. Sampled process-tree RSS stays around 1486-1487 MiB; this is not unique unified memory, GPU allocation or energy.",
    "The transactional seal, invalidation, cancellation, human-edit and rollback regressions pass, but those tests do not establish model factual quality. No default/model/backend is promoted."
  ],
